  "answer": "**No, not by recent standards. The Aussie is fairly strong against the yen over the past year, though it has slipped a little since late summer.**\n\n- **Latest:** A$1 bought ¥109.67 on 16 October 2026 (RBA 4pm rate).\n- **Over the past year:** It was ¥100.84 at the end of October 2025, so it's up about 8.8% (+¥8.83).\n- **Peak:** The high in this period was about ¥114.3 in April 2026. August 2026 ended at ¥114.54, and the daily rate was ¥114.63 on 1 September.\n- **Recent dip:** It has fallen about 4% from that early-September level, to roughly ¥110 since the end of September. It has been stable in a ¥109.4–110.7 range through October.\n\nThe yen has been weak overall, so the Aussie is up against it even though the Aussie isn't especially strong elsewhere. The AUD/USD rate is 0.6947, up from 0.6551 a year ago but down from 0.7166 in August. The trade-weighted index is 64.4, up from 61.2 a year ago and down from 66.2 in August.\n\nI only have data back to October 2025 here, so I can't compare today's level with longer-term averages.",
